{"id":"1211c401-8db1-415f-b35f-ece3401abf94","name":"Why MCP is awesome: one protocol, every tool","path":"WWW / Blog / Why MCP is awesome: one protocol, every tool","docTypeId":"e04b5e99-4f16-45e1-bb9e-3e00da70f796","templateId":"cc8b84b2-2207-4e85-93c3-9e11eb8876a0","state":"Published","domains":[],"values":[{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.TextBox","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"excerpt","value":"Before MCP, every integration was a bespoke cable. Now there is one plug both sides speak — and the content stack is no exception."},{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.BlockGrid","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"blocks","value":{"layout":{"Umbraco.BlockGrid":[{"$type":"BlockGridLayoutItem","columnSpan":12,"rowSpan":1,"areas":[],"contentKey":"9d0abbb7-f3ea-40af-8942-d1387c775f44","settingsKey":null},{"$type":"BlockGridLayoutItem","columnSpan":12,"rowSpan":1,"areas":[],"contentKey":"020534d5-456b-4188-9304-96cdade4f6bf","settingsKey":null},{"$type":"BlockGridLayoutItem","columnSpan":12,"rowSpan":1,"areas":[],"contentKey":"1eb9f248-640e-4114-8423-7c8f4f9b6190","settingsKey":null}]},"contentData":[{"contentTypeKey":"86004d4f-1443-462f-a688-24841de05a8e","key":"9d0abbb7-f3ea-40af-8942-d1387c775f44","values":[{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.TextBox","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"credit","value":"Photo: Lucian Alexe / Unsplash"},{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.RichText","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"text","value":{"markup":"<p>Every integration used to be a bespoke cable: a custom connector for the CMS, another for the CRM, another for analytics. Each one had to be designed, built, and maintained on both ends. MCP replaces that tangle with a single, open protocol — one plug that both sides already speak.</p>","blocks":{"layout":{},"contentData":[],"settingsData":[],"expose":[]}}},{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.MediaPicker3","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"image","value":[{"key":"0b7193a1-6f2f-4a2a-9274-8f4e1e2ab001","mediaKey":"61aa533d-fab8-4189-8249-89a868d9784b","mediaTypeAlias":"Image","crops":[],"focalPoint":null}]}]},{"contentTypeKey":"bf41e086-ac6a-44cb-a0a9-aaa276600da7","key":"020534d5-456b-4188-9304-96cdade4f6bf","values":[{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.RichText","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"text","value":{"markup":"<h2>The M×N problem</h2><p>Think of the old state of affairs as an M×N problem: M applications that each want to reach N tools and data sources. Without a shared protocol, the only answer was custom integrations — M×N bespoke cables, each one its own little project with its own auth quirks, its own data shapes, its own failure modes. It is the same reason USB replaced a drawer full of proprietary chargers.</p><p>MCP collapses that to M+N: an application implements the protocol once, a tool server implements it once, and they can talk. New tool? Plug it in. New client? It already knows how to listen.</p>","blocks":{"layout":{},"contentData":[],"settingsData":[],"expose":[]}}}]},{"contentTypeKey":"bf41e086-ac6a-44cb-a0a9-aaa276600da7","key":"1eb9f248-640e-4114-8423-7c8f4f9b6190","values":[{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.RichText","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"text","value":{"markup":"<h2>Why it matters for content</h2><p>For a CMS, this is quietly a big deal. An MCP server exposes the content model — document types, media, publishing — as tools and resources, so anything from an editor's assistant to a build pipeline can work with the same well-defined surface. The value is not that the protocol is clever; it is that it is <em>boring</em>: standard, open, and already implemented everywhere. That is exactly what a protocol should be.</p>","blocks":{"layout":{},"contentData":[],"settingsData":[],"expose":[]}}}]}],"settingsData":[],"expose":[{"contentKey":"9d0abbb7-f3ea-40af-8942-d1387c775f44","culture":null,"segment":null},{"contentKey":"020534d5-456b-4188-9304-96cdade4f6bf","culture":null,"segment":null},{"contentKey":"1eb9f248-640e-4114-8423-7c8f4f9b6190","culture":null,"segment":null}]}}],"urls":["/why-mcp-is-awesome-one-protocol-every-tool/"],"docType":"blogPost","template":"blogPost"}

{"id":"e93b20b6-a89e-44ec-9f37-0449764109cf","name":"Why MCP is awesome: agents that can actually help","path":"WWW / Blog / Why MCP is awesome: agents that can actually help","docTypeId":"e04b5e99-4f16-45e1-bb9e-3e00da70f796","templateId":"cc8b84b2-2207-4e85-93c3-9e11eb8876a0","state":"Published","domains":[],"values":[{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.TextBox","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"excerpt","value":"An AI assistant is only as useful as the things it can reach. MCP gives agents a safe, standard way to reach your CMS — this very post is proof."},{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.BlockGrid","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"blocks","value":{"layout":{"Umbraco.BlockGrid":[{"$type":"BlockGridLayoutItem","columnSpan":12,"rowSpan":1,"areas":[],"contentKey":"395c3d87-0a63-42fc-87bc-28d4ee9a65b7","settingsKey":null},{"$type":"BlockGridLayoutItem","columnSpan":12,"rowSpan":1,"areas":[],"contentKey":"17bffdea-618a-4ba1-a6de-b8e880ad95a8","settingsKey":null},{"$type":"BlockGridLayoutItem","columnSpan":12,"rowSpan":1,"areas":[],"contentKey":"03941582-4435-4189-941e-f9222a6e8d3e","settingsKey":null}]},"contentData":[{"contentTypeKey":"bf41e086-ac6a-44cb-a0a9-aaa276600da7","key":"395c3d87-0a63-42fc-87bc-28d4ee9a65b7","values":[{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.RichText","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"text","value":{"markup":"<p>An AI assistant is only as useful as the things it can actually reach. A brilliant writing partner that cannot see the content tree, the media library or the publish button is a partner locked out of the building. MCP changes that: it gives agents a standard, permissioned way to work with real systems — including a CMS like Umbraco.</p>","blocks":{"layout":{},"contentData":[],"settingsData":[],"expose":[]}}}]},{"contentTypeKey":"86004d4f-1443-462f-a688-24841de05a8e","key":"17bffdea-618a-4ba1-a6de-b8e880ad95a8","values":[{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.TextBox","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"credit","value":"Photo: Edge2Edge Media / Unsplash"},{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.MediaPicker3","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"image","value":[{"key":"b00cc4e2-3a2a-4a71-8a55-11e2c993ab02","mediaKey":"5c3b91a3-bd9b-46ec-930e-d9a6df9ae099","mediaTypeAlias":"Image","crops":[],"focalPoint":null}]},{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.RichText","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"text","value":{"markup":"<p>With an MCP server, the assistant does not have to guess what an <em>article</em> is or how a <em>hero</em> is put together. The content model itself tells it: here are the types, here are the fields, here is what publishes. The agent works the way an editor works — creating drafts, filling in blocks, and asking before anything goes live.</p>","blocks":{"layout":{},"contentData":[],"settingsData":[],"expose":[]}}}]},{"contentTypeKey":"bf41e086-ac6a-44cb-a0a9-aaa276600da7","key":"03941582-4435-4189-941e-f9222a6e8d3e","values":[{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.RichText","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"text","value":{"markup":"<h2>This post is the demo</h2><p>There is a neat bit of recursion here: this page, the hero on the front page, and the images in both posts were all created through Umbraco's MCP server. No bespoke script, no hand-poked database — just a protocol conversation. The assistant proposed the schema, the user confirmed each action, and the result is a site that was modeled and filled in minutes.</p><p>That is the promise of MCP: not magic, just a common language. And once everything speaks it, the interesting question stops being <em>can</em> the agent reach the CMS, and becomes <em>what should we ask it to do next</em>.</p>","blocks":{"layout":{},"contentData":[],"settingsData":[],"expose":[]}}}]}],"settingsData":[],"expose":[{"contentKey":"395c3d87-0a63-42fc-87bc-28d4ee9a65b7","culture":null,"segment":null},{"contentKey":"17bffdea-618a-4ba1-a6de-b8e880ad95a8","culture":null,"segment":null},{"contentKey":"03941582-4435-4189-941e-f9222a6e8d3e","culture":null,"segment":null}]}}],"urls":["/why-mcp-is-awesome-agents-that-can-actually-help/"],"docType":"blogPost","template":"blogPost"}

